<article id="seatopia-freshness" aria-labelledby="sf-title">
<header class="sf-intro">
<p class="eyebrow">The Seatopia Journal · Food science</p>
<h1 id="sf-title">Freshness is something<br class="desktop-break"> you preserve.</h1>
<p class="deck">What fishy odors, delicate omega-3s, and the journey from farm to fork reveal about truly exceptional seafood.</p>
<p class="byline">By Seatopia <span aria-hidden="true"> / </span> Science, sourcing &amp; a better dinner</p>
</header><figure class="hero"><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0222/9882/0682/files/salmon_on_board.jpg?v=1781859482" alt="A person slicing a salmon fillet on a wooden board in warm natural light." width="3840" loading="eager" decoding="async"><figcaption>Good seafood starts with care, long before it reaches your kitchen.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="reading intro-copy">
<p class="lead">We’ve learned to look for “fresh” seafood. Somewhere along the way, we also learned to expect it to smell fishy.</p>
<p>Those two expectations deserve a closer look.</p>
<p>A beautiful fillet on crushed ice can look like the very definition of freshness. But a display tells you surprisingly little about the time, temperatures, and handling that brought it there.</p>
<p>The more useful question is: <strong>how well has this fish been cared for?</strong></p>
<p>At Seatopia, that question connects everything: the farms we choose, the way seafood is handled, the freezing and packaging that protect it, and the independent testing that helps us understand what’s inside. The chemistry explains why.</p>
</div>
<nav class="contents reading" aria-label="In this article"><span class="eyebrow">In this article</span><a href="#sf-smell">The smell</a><a href="#sf-omega">The omega-3s</a><a href="#sf-preserve">The preservation</a><a href="#sf-standard">The standard</a></nav>
<section class="reading" id="sf-smell">
<p class="section-no">01 / Follow the chemistry</p>
<h2>That fishy smell has a story.</h2>
<p>Many marine fish naturally contain trimethylamine N-oxide, usually shortened to TMAO. After the fish dies, certain spoilage bacteria can convert it into trimethylamine, or TMA: a compound associated with that familiar fishy odor. Species, temperature, and handling all influence how spoilage develops. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-1">(FAO, n.d.)</a></p>
<div class="chemistry" role="group" aria-label="Simplified bacterial odor pathway"><div><strong>TMAO</strong><span>Naturally present in many marine fish</span></div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div><strong>TMA</strong><span>Produced by certain spoilage bacteria; fishy odor</span></div></div>
<p>That helps explain a useful piece of shopping advice: the FDA says fish should have a mild, fresh smell. Strong fishy, sour, rancid, or ammonia-like odors are reasons to pass it by. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-2">(FDA, n.d.)</a></p>
<p>Different species have different natural aromas. The point is that a powerful off-odor is something to take seriously, rather than something to disguise with lemon.</p>
<p class="small-note">A mild smell is a quality clue, not proof of safety. Safe handling and temperature control still matter.</p>
</section>
<section class="reading" id="sf-omega">
<p class="section-no">02 / Protect what makes fish valuable</p>
<h2>Omega-3s deserve careful treatment.</h2>
<p>Odor tells another story, too. Fish fats can react with oxygen in a process called lipid oxidation. In oily fish, that process contributes to rancid aromas and flavors, and it can continue during frozen storage. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-1">(FAO, n.d.)</a></p>
<p>EPA and DHA, the long-chain omega-3 fatty acids found in seafood and marine oils, are particularly susceptible to oxidation. Their chemistry makes protection from oxygen, light, and heat an important part of maintaining oil quality. As oxidation progresses, it creates primary products such as lipid peroxides and a changing mixture of secondary breakdown products. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-3">(Albert et al., 2013)</a></p>
<div class="two-paths"><div><span class="eyebrow">Bacterial spoilage</span><h3>The TMA pathway</h3><p>Certain bacteria transform TMAO into a compound associated with fishy odor.</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow">Lipid oxidation</span><h3>The rancidity pathway</h3><p>Fats react with oxygen, producing compounds that can affect aroma and flavor.</p></div></div>
<p>These are different processes. Smelling a fillet cannot tell you its EPA and DHA content or give you a complete measurement of its oxidative condition. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-1">(FAO, n.d.)</a></p>
</section>
<section class="research-band" aria-labelledby="sf-research-title"><div class="reading">
<p class="eyebrow">A closer look at the evidence</p><h2 id="sf-research-title">What fish oil studies actually show.</h2>
<p>Extracted oils give researchers a useful way to investigate oxidative quality. They also show why a single headline rarely tells the whole story.</p>
<div class="stats"><div><strong>171</strong><span>supplements examined</span></div><div><strong>49</strong><span>brands represented</span></div><div><strong>50%</strong><span>exceeded at least one voluntary oxidation limit among products successfully tested</span></div></div>
<p class="study-label">Canadian retail survey · Published 2015 · <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-4">Jackowski et al.</a></p>
<p>The survey also reported that 39% of products successfully tested for TOTOX exceeded its voluntary limit. This was a particular market sample at a particular time; it does not establish the quality of every supplement sold today.</p>
<p>Testing has limitations, too. The authors noted that flavor additives, especially citrus, can interfere with anisidine measurements. Higher readings in flavored oils may therefore reflect both oxidation and interference. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-4">(Jackowski et al., 2015)</a></p>
<p>Other research offers a different picture. A 2018 study of 23 UK encapsulated fish oil products found all had peroxide and anisidine values below the pharmacopeial limits assessed, although four exceeded the stricter industry peroxide limit of 5 mEq/kg. The benchmark used matters. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-5">(Sprague et al., 2018)</a></p>
<p><strong>The useful lesson: omega-3 quantity and oxidative quality are separate questions.</strong></p>
</div></section>
<section class="reading testing-explainer"><h2>How researchers measure oxidation.</h2>
<p>Oil testing commonly looks at more than one stage of the process:</p>
<dl class="measures"><div><dt>PV <span>Peroxide value</span></dt><dd>Measures primary oxidation products. The Canadian study used a voluntary limit of 5 mEq/kg.</dd></div><div><dt>AV <span>Anisidine value</span></dt><dd>Assesses certain secondary oxidation products. The study used a limit of 20.</dd></div><div><dt>TOTOX <span>Total oxidation value</span></dt><dd>Combines the two: TOTOX = 2 × PV + AV. The study used a limit of 26.</dd></div></dl>
<p>Peroxides can break down as oxidation advances, so a low PV alone can be misleading. These voluntary benchmarks assess oil quality; they are not established thresholds for harm in people. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-4">(Jackowski et al., 2015)</a><a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-3"> (Albert et al., 2013)</a></p>
<p>A review of oxidized marine oils highlighted gaps in the human evidence. Laboratory and animal findings do not, by themselves, demonstrate that oxidation levels measured in retail supplements cause human illness. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-3">(Albert et al., 2013)</a></p>
<p>We can make the case for thoughtful preservation without overstating the health evidence. Flavor, quality, and transparency are already worth protecting.</p>
</section>
<div class="pullquote"><p>What happened to the fish<br>matters more than a word on the label.</p></div>
<section class="reading" id="sf-preserve"><p class="section-no">03 / Preserve the quality you start with</p><h2>Rethinking the freezer.</h2>
<p>“Never frozen” tells you one thing about a fish’s history. It doesn’t tell you how quickly it was chilled, how long it traveled, or how consistently it stayed cold.</p>
<p>NOAA notes that frozen seafood can be an excellent choice because a catch may be processed and frozen promptly. A well-managed frozen supply chain offers a way to preserve quality early, rather than relying on a short refrigerated selling window. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-6">(NOAA Fisheries, 2023)</a></p>
<p>Freezing technique matters. Ice formation affects muscle structure, which is one reason researchers study how freezing and thawing methods influence fish texture. Methods that produce finer, more uniform crystals can reduce structural damage. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-7">(Alizadeh, 2012)</a></p>
<p>But freezing cannot restore quality already lost. It also doesn’t stop every change: fats can still oxidize during frozen storage. Temperature, time, and packaging remain part of the story. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-1">(FAO, n.d.)</a></p>
<p>That’s why our seafood is blast-frozen to -10°F at harvest and vacuum sealed: the aim is to protect the quality the farm starts with.</p>
</section>
<section class="split-block"><figure class=""><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0222/9882/0682/products/bakkafrost-faroe-islands-salmon-8-oz-skin-off-portions-454957.jpg?v=1748038707" alt="A vacuum-sealed Seatopia Atlantic Salmon portion with its label and QR code." width="2007" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Vacuum-sealed portions help limit oxygen exposure during frozen storage.</figcaption></figure><div><p class="eyebrow">A smaller detail with a big role</p><h2>Less air.<br>More care.</h2><p>Freezing and packaging work together. Vacuum sealing removes much of the air around a portion, while suitable packaging helps limit oxygen exposure and moisture loss.</p><p>NC State Extension recommends airtight protection to help prevent freezer burn and preserve seafood quality. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-8">(NC State Extension, n.d.)</a></p><p>At Seatopia, vacuum-sealed portions and frozen distribution are part of how we care for the fish all the way to your freezer.</p><aside class="handling"><strong>Keep the care going at home.</strong><p>Keep seafood frozen until needed. Remove vacuum-packed fish from its reduced-oxygen packaging before refrigerator thawing, and follow the product’s handling instructions. Never thaw it on the counter. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-8">(NC State Extension, n.d.)</a></p></aside></div></section>
<figure class="wide-photo"><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0222/9882/0682/files/ora-king-salmon-farm.jpg?v=1750377278" alt="Salmon farm pens in a sheltered bay surrounded by green hills." width="1800" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>An Ōra King salmon farm in New Zealand.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="reading"><p class="section-no">04 / Start long before the freezer</p><h2>Better seafood begins with better care.</h2>
<p>Preservation is one chapter in a much longer story.</p>
<p>Harvest and handling affect the quality a producer has available to preserve. Prompt dispatch, careful handling, and rapid chilling are practical measures that help protect fish quality. Rough treatment can bruise flesh; delays in chilling create opportunities for deterioration. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-8">(NC State Extension, n.d.)</a></p>
<p>At Seatopia, we look at the whole journey: how seafood is raised, what it is fed, how it is harvested, and how it is handled afterward.</p>
<p>Well-run aquaculture offers an opportunity to plan that journey. Producers can coordinate harvests, processing, and preservation, and document the steps along the way. That’s an opportunity we value, and a reason to ask detailed questions of every farm.</p>
<p>Excellent handling also exists in wild fisheries, and farming alone guarantees nothing. The practices are what count.</p>
</section>
<section class="standard-band" id="sf-standard"><div class="reading"><p class="eyebrow">The Seatopia Standard</p><h2>Some things deserve a lab report.</h2>
<p>You can appreciate a fillet’s texture and aroma. You can’t determine its mercury concentration or count its milligrams of EPA and DHA by looking at it.</p>
<p>That’s why independent testing is part of our approach to transparency. Our published program includes:</p>
<ul class="standard-list"><li><strong>Mercury.</strong> Our limit is 0.1 ppm methylmercury – a tenth of the FDA action level.</li><li><strong>Microplastics.</strong> Tested for microplastics down to 1 micron. No microplastics detected in third-party testing.</li><li><strong>Nutrition.</strong> We test and publish omega-3, protein, selenium, vitamin D and B12.</li></ul><p>Every supplier is third-party tested for nutrition, mercury and microplastics, then randomly audited to ensure consistent compliance with the Seatopia Standard.</p>
<p>“Not detected” means below the detection capability of the method used. It doesn’t prove the absolute absence of every particle in every portion. Nutrient results also describe tested samples and can vary by lot. <a class="citation" href="#sf-ref-9">(Seatopia, n.d.)</a></p>
<p>A nutrient panel and an oxidation test answer different questions. Reporting EPA and DHA content alone does not establish a fillet’s oxidative condition.</p>
<a class="text-link" href="/pages/why-clean-seafood-matters">Explore our testing program <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
</div></section>
<section class="reading"><h2>A whole-food way to enjoy omega-3s.</h2>
<p>For people who enjoy fish, whole seafood brings something a capsule cannot: a meal.</p>
<p>Alongside marine omega-3s, you’re enjoying protein, flavor, and naturally occurring nutrients whose amounts vary by species and sample. You’re also building a habit around food you look forward to eating.</p>
<p>Supplements can have a useful place, including for people who don’t eat seafood or have specific nutritional needs. The research above is a reason to care about quality, rather than a reason to dismiss an entire category.</p>
<p>Our focus is the seafood on your plate: thoughtfully raised, carefully handled, and preserved with attention to temperature, oxygen, and time.</p>
</section>
<figure class="wide-photo closing-photo"><img src="https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0222/9882/0682/files/salmon-lovers-box-9444410.jpg?v=1783077070" alt="Salmon portions cooking on a grill alongside halved stone fruit." width="1600" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The reward for thoughtful preservation: a meal worth slowing down for.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="closing reading"><p class="eyebrow">From the science to your supper</p><h2>Expect more from your seafood.</h2>
<p>A mild aroma. Beautiful texture. A clear story about where your fish came from and how it was cared for.</p>
<p>Those are better expectations than accepting a questionable smell as simply “how fish is.”</p>
<p>Thoughtful sourcing gives us quality worth protecting. Careful preservation helps us bring it home to you.</p>
<p class="closing-line">A little sea salt. A hot pan. An appetite.<br>Freshness worth sitting down for.</p>
